Projects every transcript under --projects into the store, once.

This is the only WRITING verb, and it is explicit rather than something a hook
does: the hooks keep the store current with a bounded per-invocation cap, and this
is the cold read that cap exists to avoid facing at the start of a session.

Re-running is safe and cheap. Each file's consumed prefix is fingerprinted, so an
unchanged file is skipped and a file that GREW is resumed from where it stopped —
a file that was REWRITTEN is detected and re-read from the beginning.

It is also the remedy after an upgrade. A gray-area update that changes the
store's shape rebuilds it EMPTY on the next open, and every read verb warns until
a backfill has completed; this re-reads the transcripts that still exist and
clears the warning. Sessions whose transcripts are gone do not come back.

A transcript read from the top — one the store holds no offset for, as after a
rebuild, or one that was rewritten — is read whatever its age, and the store
keeps a month by each record's own time: records older than that are read in and
removed again by the next day's prune, and a session whose every record is that
old goes with them.

It also looks for every transcript the store names, and records the ones no
longer on disk (v_transcript.gone_at). Their rows are kept: they are then the
only record of those files.

Usage:
  telepathy backfill [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for backfill

Global Flags:
      --projects string   the transcript root, which this reads and never writes (default "~/.claude/projects")
      --sessions string   where the client advertises live sessions, which is the only source of liveness (default "~/.claude/sessions")
      --store string      the catalogue database the hooks write and every read verb queries (default "$XDG_STATE_HOME/special-circumstances/catalogue/catalogue.db")
